-- allium: 3
-- live-index.allium
--
-- Scope: In-memory staging for transactions before block compaction
-- Includes: LiveIndex, LiveTable, row operations, transaction lifecycle, snapshot visibility
-- Excludes: Block storage, trie serialization, Arrow memory management
--
-- Concurrency model:
--   - Single-writer thread: all transaction operations (StartTransaction, LogPut,
--     CommitTransaction, AbortTransaction, FinishBlock, NextBlock) execute serially.
--     This is enforced structurally: whichever LogProcessor variant owns the live
--     index drives mutations sequentially. On a leader, that is the
--     LeaderLogProcessor's persister coroutine, applying on consume-back — the
--     same coroutine whether or not the database is CDC-sourced, since an
--     external source is an optional collaborator of the leader rather than a
--     processor of its own; on a follower, the FollowerLogProcessor applying
--     ReplicaMessages; during promotion, the TransitionLogProcessor. All
--     variants are single-threaded writers against the live index, so the
--     concurrency model below is the same regardless of which variant is in play.
--   - Multiple concurrent readers: snapshots can be held and read concurrently
--   - Writes don't block reads: a shared, reference-counted snapshot is a lazily-
--     advanced cache, rebuilt on read only when a reader demands a basis it does
--     not yet satisfy (gated by the reader's minimum basis); existing snapshots
--     remain valid until their last holder releases them
--
-- Pipelining (leader only) — resolve / append / consume-back / apply:
--   On a leader the pipeline has four coroutines, all children of the leader term
--   (see log-processor-lifecycle.allium). The KEY property is Raft's ReadIndex: a
--   leader applies its OWN writes to the durable live tables only once it has read
--   them BACK off its own replica log, at its own term. That consume-back is the
--   fence — it replaces the Kafka transactional producer that used to fence at
--   commit (#5817). The four coroutines:
--
--     1. RESOLVER (the persister's select loop, see db.allium). Consumes the source
--        log / ext-source txs / GC tasks. As each tx resolves, it STAGES the tx onto
--        its OWN FIFO — the resolver holds the queue, see TxResolver (a reference move
--        of the OpenTx's OWN relations out of the OpenTx, not a per-vector slice, which
--        cost real time per tx on the hot path) — and SENDS it to the append channel.
--        Staging advances the resolver's own RESOLVED head
--        (TxResolver.latest_completed_tx) so later txs in the same
--        stream resolve against it (read-your-writes). The tx is NOT yet visible to
--        external queries: it is staged and appended, but not yet consumed back and
--        applied to the durable index.
--
--     2. APPEND PUMP. Drains the append channel and, per item, SERIALIZES the tx's
--        replica message off the resolver (the costly Arrow-IPC step, kept off the
--        resolver's hot path) and APPENDS it to the replica log via a PLAIN
--        (non-transactional) append — Log.appendMessage, no producer transaction,
--        no fencing token, no initTransactions. Every appended record is STAMPED
--        with the leader's term. Control messages (block boundaries, no-ops, tries
--        added/deleted) are appended verbatim, also term-stamped. An append fault
--        propagates and tears the term down. The pump only BORROWS a tx to serialize
--        it; the resolver owns and frees it.
--
--     3. CONSUME PUMP. Tails the leader's OWN replica log from the transition replay
--        target (a separate consumer from the source-log group subscription that
--        drives leader election, so it doesn't interfere with it) and posts each
--        record onto a bounded consume-back channel.
--
--     4. The RESOLVER's select loop also SERVICES the consume-back channel — this is
--        the APPLY / ReadIndex step. Applying a record (see ApplyRecord):
--          - FENCE BY TERM. A record whose term is GREATER than the leader's own term
--            means a newer leader has superseded us → the leader RESIGNS (its
--            unconfirmed writes are never acknowledged; the transport re-follows on
--            the next rebalance). A record with a LOWER term is discarded (still
--            advancing the consume cursor). An EQUAL-term record is our own — terms
--            are unique per leader.
--          - For our own ResolvedTx: import from the QUEUE HEAD (we still hold its
--            relations, so no re-materialisation), advancing the durable basis
--            (LiveIndex.latest_completed_tx) and the consume cursor, notifying
--            watchers, then free the head. THIS is where a committed tx becomes
--            queryable, and where an ext-source caller's durability handle completes.
--          - For our own BlockBoundary: trigger the block upload.
--
--   Resolution and apply share one coroutine (the select loop), so a tx cannot
--   interleave a partial state. The shared external snapshot is NOT refreshed on
--   apply: it is a lazily-advanced cache, rebuilt on the next demanding read (see
--   RefreshSharedSnapshot).
--
--   Followers and the transition processor have no resolver (hence no queue) and no
--   consume-back: the messages they import are already durable, so they advance
--   LiveIndex.latest_completed_tx directly.
--
--   Block cut — resolve-side gauge, then PAUSE:
--   The block boundary is cut off a RESOLVE-side row gauge (rows_since_block),
--   seeded from the rows already applied into the open block (LiveIndex.block_row_count)
--   so a new leader inheriting a partially-filled block from replay cuts it at the same
--   point the old leader would have (else block sizes drift across restarts). It is cut
--   on the resolve side, AHEAD of the applied/durable count (LiveIndex.is_full lags —
--   it reflects only consumed-back txs). When the gauge crosses rows_per_block the
--   resolver injects a BlockBoundary (in resolution order, so it lands after this
--   block's txs and before the next block's) and PAUSES resolution — the source-log,
--   ext-source and GC select arms are EXCLUDED — until the boundary is read back and its
--   block uploaded. This pause keeps the follower's bounded pending-block buffer from
--   overflowing (no tx interleaves between the boundary and its upload). The pause is
--   deliberately the status quo; block double-buffering is separate future work (#4495).
--
--   Two heads, two homes — callers choose:
--     - the RESOLVED head, TxResolver.latest_completed_tx: the highest tx RESOLVED (and
--       so staged). Advanced at STAGE, on the resolve side. Drives resolution — next
--       tx_id, system-time smoothing, read-your-writes.
--     - the DURABLE head, LiveIndex.latest_completed_tx: the highest tx APPLIED, i.e.
--       consumed back off the replica log and imported. Advanced at APPLY. The query
--       basis.
--   Resolved LEADS; durable LAGS it by exactly everything queued but not yet applied.
--   ("Applied" throughout this file means the far end — imported into the durable live
--   index on consume-back, see ApplyResolvedTx — never merely staged.)
--
--   The queue is NOT a field on LiveIndex, and NOT standing beside the resolver: the
--   RESOLVER holds it. That is forced by read-your-writes — a tx resolving now must see
--   every predecessor not yet applied, so whoever resolves has to hold the staged txs.
--   The resolver in turn is standing state the leader (LeaderLogProcessor) owns: the
--   leader creates it at construction and closes it at teardown, once its persister
--   coroutine is joined (see CloseResolver). LiveIndex stays the durable layer and stays
--   resolver-agnostic. Consume-back imports into the durable LiveIndex under its
--   existing write lock.
--
--   Ownership: the RESOLVER OWNS the queue and its staged txs (their table relations).
--   It frees each at APPLY (removeHead — once imported) or at TEARDOWN (close). The
--   append pump only borrows a tx to serialize it; it never frees one. A record already
--   off the queue head (during apply) is freed by the apply path itself, on ANY throw,
--   so teardown's fail-pending can't reach it and double-close it.
--
--   Per-tx durability handles: each staged EXTERNAL-SOURCE tx carries a nullable
--   durability handle (ResolvedTx.pending, a deferred completed with the tx's result);
--   source-log txs carry none (the source log is the durability record for those). The
--   handle is completed on CONSUME-BACK, once the tx is imported — durably replicated
--   AND confirmed unfenced (ReadIndex). executeTx = submit + await the handle;
--   submitTx = fire-and-forget. Failure paths MUST fail the handle so no caller waits
--   forever:
--     (a) a writer/stage throw BEFORE the tx is staged fails it at the throw site;
--     (b) staged txs that will never be applied are failed when the RESOLVER EXITS
--         (fault or cancellation) — the fail-pending leg (resolver.failPending) — before
--         the channels close;
--     (c) an undelivered task's handle is cancelled by the channel's undelivered-element
--         hook;
--     (d) a submit posted AFTER resolver death throws the close cause at the send.
--
-- Critical invariant: External queries see only durably-replicated transactions
--   (LiveIndex.latest_completed_tx) — those the leader has read BACK off its own
--   replica log and applied. A query returned to a user must never include a tx that
--   could vanish if the leader crashed before its replica-log write landed, nor a tx a
--   newer-term leader superseded. Resolution sees durable ⊕ staged-in-queue ⊕ the tx's
--   own writes.

------------------------------------------------------------
-- External Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

external entity TransactionKey {
    tx_id: Integer
    system_time: Timestamp
}

external entity TableRef {
    dbName: String
    schemaName: String
    tableName: String
}

external entity IID {}   -- 16-byte entity identifier

external entity Document {}   -- the actual row data for puts

external entity MemorySegment {}
    -- A slice of an Arrow relation paired with the MemoryHashTrie indexing
    -- its IID column. The slice is taken at snapshot creation and is
    -- unaffected by subsequent writes to the source relation.
    -- See: xtdb.segment.MemorySegment, memory-hash-trie.allium.

-- A per-tx durability handle: a deferred completed with the tx's result once the tx
-- has been CONSUMED BACK and imported into the durable live tables (durably replicated
-- AND confirmed unfenced — Raft's ReadIndex). Carried by external-source txs only
-- (ResolvedTx.pending); source-log txs carry none. executeTx awaits this handle before
-- returning; submitTx does not (fire-and-forget). The resolver completes it on apply
-- (see ApplyResolvedTx), or FAILS it on any path where the tx will never be applied (a
-- stage-time throw, resolver exit with the tx still staged, an undelivered task, or a
-- submit after resolver death) — see the header "Per-tx durability handles". Modelled
-- as a Deferred<TransactionResult> in the implementation.
external entity DurabilityHandle {}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Value Types
------------------------------------------------------------

value ValidTimeRange {
    from: Timestamp
    to: Timestamp
}

value Row {
    -- `Row` is an abstraction used by the rules below to describe commit
    -- semantics. The actual storage is an Arrow relation (not a row set): each
    -- LiveTable holds a `liveRelation` and each in-flight table-tx holds a
    -- `txRelation`, with a MemoryHashTrie built over the IID column. The `op`
    -- column is an Arrow dense union with three legs: put, delete, erase.
    -- See: LiveTable.kt, OpenTx.kt.
    iid: IID
    system_from: Timestamp
    valid_time: ValidTimeRange
    op: Op
}

value Op {
    -- Arrow DenseUnion: exactly one leg is populated per row. Modelled as three
    -- optional legs (rather than a checker-enforced sum type) only because the
    -- installed CLI predates the union construct this file targets; the "exactly one
    -- populated" constraint is the real rule.
    put: Put?
    delete: Delete?
    erase: Erase?
}

value Put {
    -- The put leg is a struct containing the document columns.
    doc: Document
}

value Delete { }   -- null marker in the "delete" leg
value Erase { }    -- null marker in the "erase" leg

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Entities
------------------------------------------------------------

entity LiveIndex {
    -- DURABLE head / QUERY BASIS: the highest tx APPLIED into the durable live tables —
    -- the highest tx the leader has read BACK off its own replica log and imported
    -- (ReadIndex). shared_snap is built with basis_tx = latest_completed_tx; external
    -- queries open at it. On a leader it is advanced by ApplyResolvedTx on consume-back
    -- (so it LAGS the resolver's RESOLVED head by everything staged but not yet consumed
    -- back and applied); on a follower/transition by importTx (every imported message is
    -- already durable, so there is no lag).
    --
    -- The RESOLVED head (which drives resolution — next external-source tx_id is
    -- head.tx_id + 1, and system-time smoothing reads it) is NOT here: on a
    -- leader the RESOLVER maintains its own latest_completed_tx over the queue it
    -- holds (see TxResolver). LiveIndex is resolver-agnostic — it knows only the
    -- durable layer. Callers choose which head to read — resolution reads the
    -- resolver's RESOLVED head, external queries read this DURABLE one.
    latest_completed_tx: TransactionKey?

    rows_per_block: Integer

    -- Rows already applied into the currently-open block. Seeds the resolver's
    -- rows_since_block gauge on leader startup so a new leader inheriting a
    -- partially-filled block from replay cuts it at the same point the old leader
    -- would have. See the header "Block cut".
    block_row_count: Integer

    -- Durable live tables: hold only applied (consumed-back, durably-replicated) rows.
    tables: LiveTable with index = this

    -- Current shared external snapshot: a lazily-advanced cache, NOT refreshed
    -- eagerly. It is rebuilt (see RefreshSharedSnapshot) only when a reader
    -- requests a basis this cache does not satisfy; apply and NextBlock leave
    -- it stale. External readers retain this snapshot when calling
    -- OpenSnapshot(index, min_basis); see OpenExternalSnapshot. Its basis is
    -- latest_completed_tx (durable) as of the last rebuild.
    shared_snap: Snapshot

    row_count: tables.sum(t => t.row_count)
    is_full: row_count >= rows_per_block
}

entity TxResolver {
    -- The leader's transaction RESOLVER: it turns a source-log message, a database
    -- attach/detach or an external source's writer into a resolved tx, and holds the
    -- FIFO of resolved-but-not-yet-applied txs until the leader has applied them.
    --
    -- The queue lives HERE, on the resolver, rather than beside it, because of
    -- READ-YOUR-WRITES: a tx resolving now must see every predecessor still queued
    -- (durable ⊕ staged ⊕ its own writes), so whoever resolves has to hold the staged
    -- txs. Resolution and the queue are one concern, not two.
    --
    -- NOT a field on LiveIndex: this is standing state the leader (LeaderLogProcessor)
    -- owns — the leader creates the resolver at construction and closes it at teardown,
    -- once its persister coroutine is joined (see CloseResolver). The resolver is driven
    -- only from that persister coroutine, its sole accessor, so it is single-threaded by
    -- confinement and needs no lock. Seeded at the durable head when created; absent on
    -- followers and the transition processor, which only import already-durable messages.
    --
    -- The resolver OWNS its staged txs — their table relations. Each is freed at APPLY
    -- (RemoveHead — once imported) or at TEARDOWN (close). The append pump only borrows
    -- a tx to serialize it; it never frees one. Because the resolver holds the resolved
    -- txs' relations directly, the leader imports them on consume-back with NO
    -- re-materialisation (it doesn't re-load them from the replica log — the replica
    -- record it reads back only confirms durability + term).
    --
    -- The durable LiveIndex this resolver pipelines into. The link is one-way: the
    -- resolver references the index, but the index does not reference the resolver (it
    -- stays resolver-agnostic).
    index: LiveIndex

    -- RESOLVED head: the highest tx RESOLVED (and so staged) — NOT the highest applied.
    -- Drives RESOLUTION — next external-source tx_id is latest_completed_tx.tx_id + 1,
    -- system-time smoothing reads it, and resolution layers read-your-writes over the
    -- staged txs. Seeded from index.latest_completed_tx (the durable head) when the
    -- resolver is created, then advanced at STAGE — on the RESOLVE side (see
    -- CommitTransaction / AbortTransaction), not at apply. So it LEADS the durable head:
    -- always >= index.latest_completed_tx, by exactly the txs staged but not yet applied.
    latest_completed_tx: TransactionKey?

    -- The staged txs, oldest→newest (resolution order). Stage appends; ApplyResolvedTx
    -- removes the head after importing it. Resolution layers these as read-your-writes
    -- segments (see StagedSegments.for).
    staged: StagedTx with resolver = this

    -- Whether the queue is empty: ApplyResolvedTx requires a head to import.
    is_empty: staged.count = 0
}

entity StagedTx {
    -- One resolved tx held in the resolver's queue: no longer OPEN (it can't be written
    -- to or queried), retaining its per-table rows so ApplyResolvedTx can import it into
    -- the durable live tables on consume-back, and so later txs resolving behind it read
    -- it (read-your-writes). Its relations are the OpenTx's own, ownership moved out at
    -- Stage (a reference move — see the header "Ownership"); the resolver closes the
    -- (now table-less) OpenTx right after staging. Freed at RemoveHead (apply) or
    -- teardown (CloseResolver).
    --
    -- StagedTx carries no replica-log position: it is serialized into a replica message
    -- by the append pump, and its durability is confirmed when the leader reads that
    -- message back at its own term (ReadIndex). It holds the row data (for
    -- read-your-writes + import) plus, for an external-source tx, the caller's
    -- durability handle.
    --
    -- durability (ResolvedTx.pending): the caller's per-tx durability handle, or null.
    -- Non-null for external-source txs (executeTx/submitTx), null for source-log txs.
    -- The resolver completes it on APPLY (ApplyResolvedTx) — once the tx is consumed
    -- back and imported — or fails it on a path where the tx will never be applied (see
    -- the header "Per-tx durability handles" and FailPendingHandles / CloseResolver).
    resolver: TxResolver
    tx_key: TransactionKey
    durability: DurabilityHandle?
    tables: StagedTxTable with staged_tx = this
}

entity StagedTxTable {
    -- One tx's rows for one table, imported into the matching durable LiveTable
    -- when the tx is applied (see ApplyResolvedTx).
    staged_tx: StagedTx
    table_ref: TableRef
    rows: Set<Row>

    row_count: rows.count
}

entity LiveTable {
    -- LiveTables are cleared (removed) by NextBlock, not just emptied
    index: LiveIndex
    table_ref: TableRef
    rows: Set<Row>

    row_count: rows.count
}

entity OpenTx {
    index: LiveIndex

    -- The resolver this tx resolves against. A resolving tx reads durable ⊕ the
    -- resolver's staged txs ⊕ its own writes, so it carries the resolver (which hands
    -- itself to each tx it opens). Always present: open transactions exist only on a
    -- leader, where the resolver exists.
    resolver: TxResolver

    tx_key: TransactionKey
    status: active | committed | aborted

    transitions status {
        active -> committed
        active -> aborted
        terminal: committed, aborted
    }

    table_txs: LiveTableTx with transaction = this

    system_from: tx_key.system_time
}

entity LiveTableTx {
    transaction: OpenTx
    table_ref: TableRef
    live_table: LiveTable?   -- null if table is new
    is_new_table: live_table = null

    rows: Set<Row>           -- uncommitted rows in this tx
    row_count: rows.count
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Snapshots (visibility)
--
-- A Snapshot is a point-in-time view over the live index. It is composed of
-- per-table TableSnapshots, each holding a MemorySegment: a sliced live
-- relation plus the MemoryHashTrie indexing it. When opened from within an
-- active transaction, a TableSnapshot also layers an optional tx segment
-- (sliced from the OpenTx.Table's txRelation) on top of the live segment, so
-- queries inside the tx see committed rows + their own uncommitted rows.
--
-- Consistency guarantees:
--   - A snapshot's underlying segments are sliced at creation and do not
--     observe later writes
--   - Commits are atomic: all tx rows become visible together, or none
--   - Multiple snapshots can be held concurrently without interference
--   - Writes don't block reads: a shared external snapshot is rebuilt lazily on
--     read (only when a reader demands a basis it does not yet satisfy), and
--     existing snapshots remain valid until released
--
-- Sharing and reference counting:
--   - External snapshots are shared: the live index publishes a single
--     current shared snapshot, and each external opener retains it
--   - A snapshot is released by its holder via close(); the underlying
--     segments are freed when the last holder releases
--   - Transaction snapshots are per-OpenTx and not shared
------------------------------------------------------------

entity Snapshot {
    basis_tx: TransactionKey?

    table_snapshots: TableSnapshot with snapshot = this

    -- External snapshots are shared and reference-counted; concurrent
    -- readers each hold one retention on the current shared snapshot.
    ref_count: Integer

    -- Derived: which tables are visible in this snapshot
    visible_tables: table_snapshots.map(ts => ts.table_ref)
}

entity TableSnapshot {
    snapshot: Snapshot
    table_ref: TableRef

    -- Live segment: slice of the LiveTable's (DURABLE) liveRelation paired with
    -- its MemoryHashTrie. Absent when the table has no durable data at snapshot
    -- time. This is the only layer on external snapshots.
    live_segment: MemorySegment?

    -- Staged segments: slices of this table's staged-in-queue rows, layered on top of
    -- live_segment, oldest→newest — one per StagedTx in the queue that touched this
    -- table (its matching StagedTxTable). A just-staged tx's rows live ONLY here until
    -- consumed back and applied. Empty on external snapshots (which see durable data
    -- only); populated only on transaction snapshots so a resolving tx reads staged
    -- prior txs (read-your-writes). See StagedSegments.for.
    staged_segments: Set<MemorySegment>

    -- Tx segment: slice of the enclosing OpenTx.Table's txRelation paired with
    -- its MemoryHashTrie. Present only on transaction snapshots where the tx
    -- has written rows for this table. Layered on top of staged_segments so a
    -- query inside the tx sees durable + staged rows + its own uncommitted rows.
    tx_segment: MemorySegment?
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Config
------------------------------------------------------------

config {
    -- The block-sizing bound (IndexerConfig.rowsPerBlock). The resolver's block-cut
    -- gauge (rows_since_block) crosses this to inject a BlockBoundary. See the header
    -- "Block cut".
    rows_per_block: Integer = 102_400
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Defaults
------------------------------------------------------------

default ValidTimeRange erase_valid_time = { from: MIN_TIMESTAMP, to: MAX_TIMESTAMP }

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: Transaction lifecycle
------------------------------------------------------------

rule StartTransaction {
    -- Started by the resolver, which hands itself to the tx so the tx can resolve
    -- against durable ⊕ its staged txs (read-your-writes).
    when: StartTransaction(index, resolver, tx_key)

    requires: resolver.index = index

    ensures: OpenTx.created(
        index: index,
        resolver: resolver,
        tx_key: tx_key,
        status: active
    )
}

rule GetTableTx {
    -- Idempotent: returns the existing LiveTableTx if one already exists for this tx and table.
    -- See: live_index.clj (computeIfAbsent on table-txs)
    when: GetTableTx(tx, table_ref)

    requires: tx.status = active

    let existing_table_tx = tx.table_txs.find(tt => tt.table_ref = table_ref)

    ensures:
        if existing_table_tx != null:
            existing_table_tx
        else:
            let existing_table = tx.index.tables.find(t => t.table_ref = table_ref)
            LiveTableTx.created(
                transaction: tx,
                table_ref: table_ref,
                live_table: existing_table,
                rows: {}
            )
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: Row operations
------------------------------------------------------------

rule LogPut {
    when: LogPut(table_tx, iid, valid_time, doc)

    requires: table_tx.transaction.status = active

    ensures: table_tx.rows.add(Row{
        iid: iid,
        system_from: table_tx.transaction.system_from,
        valid_time: valid_time,
        op: Op{ put: Put{ doc: doc } }
    })
}

rule LogDelete {
    when: LogDelete(table_tx, iid, valid_time)

    requires: table_tx.transaction.status = active

    ensures: table_tx.rows.add(Row{
        iid: iid,
        system_from: table_tx.transaction.system_from,
        valid_time: valid_time,
        op: Op{ delete: Delete{} }
    })
}

rule LogErase {
    when: LogErase(table_tx, iid)

    requires: table_tx.transaction.status = active

    ensures: table_tx.rows.add(Row{
        iid: iid,
        system_from: table_tx.transaction.system_from,
        valid_time: erase_valid_time,
        op: Op{ erase: Erase{} }
    })
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: Commit and Abort (RESOLVE side — stage + append, not apply)
------------------------------------------------------------

rule CommitTransaction {
    -- The resolver STAGES a committed tx onto its queue and advances its
    -- RESOLVED head (TxResolver.latest_completed_tx). Staging takes ownership of
    -- the tx's table relations (a reference move — see the header "Ownership"); the
    -- (now table-less) OpenTx is closed right after. The staged tx is then SENT to the
    -- append pump, which serializes it and plain-appends it to the replica log.
    --
    -- It does NOT advance the DURABLE head (LiveIndex.latest_completed_tx) and does
    -- NOT refresh the external shared snapshot — those happen only when the leader
    -- CONSUMES the tx BACK off its own replica log and imports it (see ApplyResolvedTx,
    -- the ReadIndex step). Later txs in the same stream resolve against the resolver's
    -- queue (read-your-writes), but external queries cannot yet see it.
    --
    -- durability is the caller's per-tx durability handle for an EXTERNAL-SOURCE tx
    -- (executeTx/submitTx), or omitted (null) for a source-log tx. It is stored on the
    -- StagedTx and completed on APPLY (see ApplyResolvedTx), or failed on a
    -- non-applying path (see the header "Per-tx durability handles").
    when: CommitTransaction(tx, durability?)

    requires: tx.status = active

    let resolver = tx.resolver

    ensures: tx.status = committed
    ensures: resolver.latest_completed_tx = tx.tx_key

    -- Stage the tx: record it at the tail in resolution order, retaining its per-table
    -- rows so ApplyResolvedTx can import it on consume-back and so later txs read it.
    -- Tables with no tx rows are skipped.
    ensures:
        let staged_tx = StagedTx.created(
            resolver: resolver,
            tx_key: tx.tx_key,
            durability: durability
        )
        for table_tx in tx.table_txs where table_tx.rows.count > 0:
            StagedTxTable.created(
                staged_tx: staged_tx,
                table_ref: table_tx.table_ref,
                rows: table_tx.rows
            )
}

rule AbortTransaction {
    when: AbortTransaction(tx)

    requires: tx.status = active

    ensures: tx.status = aborted
    -- The RESOLVED head tracks "resolved up to", not "successfully committed up
    -- to" — even aborted transactions consume a tx_id and advance the resolver's
    -- marker, so the next tx_id is correct. An aborted tx is still staged (as an
    -- abort row) and appended, so it too is consumed back and applied.
    ensures: tx.resolver.latest_completed_tx = tx.tx_key
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: Consume-back / apply (ReadIndex — where a tx becomes durable)
--
-- The RESOLVER's select loop services the consume-back channel the consume pump
-- feeds. This is Raft's ReadIndex: the leader applies its own writes only after
-- reading them back off its own replica log, at its own term. The term on read-back
-- is the fence (see ApplyRecord); it replaces the Kafka transactional producer that
-- used to fence at commit (#5817).
------------------------------------------------------------

rule ApplyRecord {
    -- Apply one record read back off the leader's OWN replica log. Fences by TERM:
    --   - term GREATER than ours: a newer leader has superseded us → RESIGN. Modelled
    --     as LeaderSuperseded; the leader term fails (its unconfirmed staged txs are
    --     failed by the fail-pending leg, see FailPendingHandles), and the transport
    --     re-follows on the next rebalance. This is EXPECTED — not a query-facing fault,
    --     so it does NOT poison the watchers.
    --   - term LOWER than ours: shouldn't appear past our replay target; discarded
    --     defensively, still advancing the consume cursor (RecordDiscarded).
    --   - term EQUAL to ours (the common case; terms are unique per leader, so an
    --     equal-term record IS our own): dispatched to ApplyResolvedTx (for a tx) or
    --     the block-upload path (for a BlockBoundary), etc.
    when: ApplyRecord(resolver, record_term, our_term)

    ensures:
        if record_term > our_term:
            LeaderSuperseded(resolver: resolver)
        else if record_term < our_term:
            RecordDiscarded(resolver: resolver)
        else:
            RecordApplied(resolver: resolver)
}

rule ApplyResolvedTx {
    -- The leader reads its OWN staged tx back at its own term and IMPORTS it — this is
    -- the point at which a committed tx becomes queryable (durably replicated AND
    -- confirmed unfenced, ReadIndex). It imports from the QUEUE HEAD (peeking the front
    -- must match the consumed tx_id — a mismatch is a fault), which still holds the
    -- tx's relations, so there is NO re-materialisation from the replica record. On any
    -- throw here the head — already off the queue — has its durability handle failed and
    -- its buffers freed by the apply path itself (so teardown's fail-pending can't reach
    -- it). Notifying watchers happens per applied tx.
    --
    -- The DURABLE head advance ALONE makes the newly-durable tx queryable; the shared
    -- external snapshot is a lazily-advanced cache, rebuilt on the next demanding read
    -- (see RefreshSharedSnapshot), NOT refreshed here.
    --
    -- The caller's per-tx durability handle (external-source txs only) is completed HERE,
    -- as the last step — this is where an executeTx caller finally unblocks, after (not
    -- before) the tx is durable. Source-log txs carry no handle.
    when: RecordApplied(resolver)

    requires: not resolver.is_empty

    let index = resolver.index
    let staged_tx = resolver.staged.first

    ensures:
        -- Import this tx's rows into the (possibly new) durable live tables.
        for staged_table in staged_tx.tables:
            let live_table =
                let existing = index.tables.find(t => t.table_ref = staged_table.table_ref)
                if existing != null:
                    existing
                else:
                    LiveTable.created(
                        index: index,
                        table_ref: staged_table.table_ref,
                        rows: {}
                    )
            live_table.rows.addAll(staged_table.rows)

        -- Advance the DURABLE head to this tx (the durable live index's own
        -- latest_completed_tx) — the RESOLVED head passed this tx back at stage time.
        -- This alone makes the newly-durable tx queryable.
        index.latest_completed_tx = staged_tx.tx_key

        -- Complete an external-source tx's durability handle now that it is durable
        -- (source-log txs carry none).
        if staged_tx.durability != null:
            HandleCompleted(staged_tx.durability)

        -- Remove the head — AFTER importing it — and free it (the resolver owns it).
        not exists staged_tx
}

rule FailPendingHandles {
    -- The FAIL-PENDING leg (header legs b/c). When the RESOLVER EXITS — a fault, a
    -- supersession (LeaderSuperseded), or a teardown cancellation — every durability
    -- handle of a still-staged tx whose tx will NEVER be applied is FAILED with the exit
    -- cause, so no executeTx caller waits forever. This runs at resolver exit, BEFORE the
    -- channels close (resolver.failPending). Source-log txs carry no handle, so they
    -- contribute nothing. An undelivered task's handle (leg c) is failed by the channel's
    -- undelivered-element hook rather than here; a submit after resolver death (leg d)
    -- throws the close cause at the submit site. See the header "Per-tx durability
    -- handles".
    when: ResolverExited(resolver, cause)

    ensures:
        for staged_tx in resolver.staged where staged_tx.durability != null:
            HandleFailed(staged_tx.durability, cause)
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: Snapshot visibility
--
-- CRITICAL: External queries see only DURABLY-REPLICATED transactions
-- (LiveIndex.latest_completed_tx) — durable live segments only, no staged-in-queue
-- rows. Resolution (queries inside an in-flight tx) sees durable ⊕ staged ⊕ the
-- tx's own writes.
------------------------------------------------------------

rule OpenExternalSnapshot {
    -- Called by queries outside any transaction. min_basis is the caller's
    -- minimum read basis (a system-time); it may be absent (null).
    --
    -- The index holds a single shared Snapshot whose basis is latest_completed_tx
    -- (durable) as of its last rebuild and whose layers are the DURABLE live
    -- segments only — no staged-in-queue rows. It is a lazily-advanced cache, NOT
    -- refreshed on apply or NextBlock. If the caller has no minimum basis, or the
    -- cached snapshot's basis already satisfies it, the current cache is retained and
    -- returned as-is; otherwise it is rebuilt to the current durable basis first
    -- (see RefreshSharedSnapshot). The awaited/durable basis guarantees
    -- latest_completed_tx >= min_basis by the time this runs, so a rebuild can
    -- always reach a satisfying basis. Each external opener retains the shared
    -- snapshot and releases it on close; the underlying segments are freed when
    -- the last holder releases.
    when: OpenSnapshot(index, min_basis?)

    ensures:
        -- Rebuild only when the cache is too stale for the requested basis.
        if min_basis != null and index.shared_snap.basis_tx.system_time < min_basis:
            RefreshSharedSnapshot(index)
        let snap = index.shared_snap
        snap.ref_count = snap.ref_count + 1
        snap   -- returned to the caller; close() decrements ref_count
}

rule OpenTransactionSnapshot {
    -- Called by queries within an in-flight resolving transaction
    -- (e.g. UPDATE...WHERE). Only active transactions can open snapshots;
    -- committed/aborted txs are closed. Transaction snapshots are built
    -- per-OpenTx and are NOT shared.
    --
    -- A resolving tx must read all prior txs whether DURABLE or merely STAGED,
    -- plus its own uncommitted writes. So each TableSnapshot layers, bottom to
    -- top: the durable live segment ⊕ the staged segments (the resolver's StagedTxTables
    -- for this table, oldest→newest) ⊕ the tx's own segment.
    when: OpenSnapshot(tx)

    requires: tx.status = active

    ensures:
        let snap = Snapshot.created(basis_tx: tx.tx_key, ref_count: 1)
        let resolver = tx.resolver

        -- One TableSnapshot per table touched by the tx: durable live segment
        -- from the existing LiveTable (if any), the staged segments for the
        -- table (the resolver's StagedTxTables oldest→newest), and the tx segment
        -- from this OpenTx.
        for table_tx in tx.table_txs:
            let live_seg = if table_tx.live_table != null: MemorySegment.for(table_tx.live_table) else: null
            let tx_seg = if table_tx.rows.count > 0: MemorySegment.for(table_tx) else: null
            TableSnapshot.created(
                snapshot: snap,
                table_ref: table_tx.table_ref,
                live_segment: live_seg,
                staged_segments: StagedSegments.for(resolver, table_tx.table_ref),
                tx_segment: tx_seg
            )

        -- Plus the remaining live tables the tx hasn't touched: durable live
        -- segment plus any staged segments for the table, no tx segment.
        for table in tx.index.tables
                where not tx.table_txs.any(tt => tt.table_ref = table.table_ref):
            TableSnapshot.created(
                snapshot: snap,
                table_ref: table.table_ref,
                live_segment: MemorySegment.for(table),
                staged_segments: StagedSegments.for(resolver, table.table_ref),
                tx_segment: null
            )

        snap
}

rule RefreshSharedSnapshot {
    -- The rebuild PRIMITIVE for the shared external snapshot. NOT triggered by
    -- apply or NextBlock (the cache is advanced lazily). It is invoked by
    -- OpenExternalSnapshot when the cached snapshot is too stale for the
    -- requested basis, and out-of-band by compaction (which mutates the
    -- trie-catalog without advancing latest_completed_tx). The index atomically
    -- swaps its shared snapshot for a freshly-built one reflecting the current
    -- durable state, and releases its own retention on the previous shared
    -- snapshot. Readers already holding the previous shared snapshot retain it
    -- independently.
    when: RefreshSharedSnapshot(index)

    ensures:
        let old_snap = index.shared_snap
        let new_snap = Snapshot.created(
            -- DURABLE basis: the external snapshot sees only durably-replicated
            -- txs, never the resolver's RESOLVED head (staged, not yet applied).
            basis_tx: index.latest_completed_tx,
            ref_count: 1   -- the index's own retention on the shared snap
        )
        for table in index.tables:
            TableSnapshot.created(
                snapshot: new_snap,
                table_ref: table.table_ref,
                live_segment: MemorySegment.for(table),
                -- External snapshot: durable data only, no staged layers.
                staged_segments: {},
                tx_segment: null
            )
        index.shared_snap = new_snap
        -- Decrement the index's retention on the old shared snap; it is
        -- freed once no external readers are still holding it.
        old_snap.ref_count = old_snap.ref_count - 1
}

------------------------------------------------------------
-- Rules: Block lifecycle
------------------------------------------------------------

rule CutBlock {
    -- The RESOLVER cuts a block off its resolve-side row gauge (rows_since_block):
    -- when the gauge crosses rows_per_block after staging a tx, it injects a
    -- BlockBoundary (in resolution order, via the append pump — a plain, term-stamped
    -- append) and PAUSES resolution. The boundary lands after this block's txs and
    -- before the next block's. Resolution stays paused (the source-log, ext-source and
    -- GC arms are excluded from the select) until the boundary is read back and its
    -- block uploaded, so the follower's bounded pending-block buffer can't overflow.
    -- The gauge is reset to 0. See the header "Block cut".
    when: CutBlock(index, block_idx, latest_processed_msg_id)

    ensures: BlockBoundaryAppended(index, block_idx, latest_processed_msg_id)
}

rule FinishBlock {
    -- Triggered on consume-back when the leader reads its own BlockBoundary back at
    -- its own term (or, on a follower/transition, when it reads a durable boundary).
    -- The live index now holds exactly this block's txs (by log order), so its durable
    -- contents are written to block storage, then a BlockUploaded is appended (which,
    -- read back, resumes any resolution paused by CutBlock). Rows remain in memory
    -- until NextBlock.
    --
    -- Because resolution paused at the boundary and stays paused until this upload,
    -- nothing was staged-and-applied between the boundary and here — a durable L0 block
    -- never contains rows from a later block.
    when: FinishBlock(index, block_idx)

    -- Writes current (durable) live table contents to block storage.
    ensures: BlockWritten(index, block_idx)
}

rule CloseResolver {
    -- Leader → follower demotion (and teardown) closes the resolver — the leader owns it
    -- and does this in its own close. Phase 1 cancels-and-joins the resolver + pump
    -- coroutine tree (so nothing live still touches the queue) and, on resolver exit,
    -- fails the pending durability handles (FailPendingHandles) BEFORE the channels
    -- close, so no executeTx caller waits forever. Phase 2 (this) closes the resolver
    -- synchronously, once its persister coroutine is joined: it frees the queue — any
    -- un-applied staged txs' buffers — and then its own allocator. Any staged-but-not-
    -- yet-applied txs are discarded (they were never visible to external queries) and
    -- will be re-read from the source log and re-resolved by the next leader. The
    -- durable LiveIndex is untouched. See log-processor-lifecycle.allium.
    --
    -- The FAIL-PENDING leg does NOT happen here — the handles are already failed at
    -- resolver exit (phase 1). This rule only reclaims the buffers.
    when: CloseResolver(resolver)

    ensures:
        for staged_tx in resolver.staged:
            not exists staged_tx
        not exists resolver
}

rule NextBlock {
    when: NextBlock(index)

    -- Remove all live tables (their data is now in block storage)
    -- Tables will be recreated on demand by future transactions
    ensures: index.tables = {}

    -- The shared snapshot is NOT refreshed here. A subsequent reader whose basis
    -- the (now stale) cache still satisfies may reuse it: its sliced segments are
    -- independent of the cleared tables and remain valid. A reader demanding a
    -- newer basis rebuilds the cache (see OpenExternalSnapshot).
}
